790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:26:**Two shared host types, `Closure` and `Continuation`, each wrapping the kernel's existing payload map plus an owner tag, minted only by kernels, opaque to guest primitives, with structural equality.**\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:28:- **One namespace, `yin.vm.value`,** modelled on `yin.vm.effect` (`effect.cljc`): `deftype Closure [owner payload]`, `deftype Continuation [owner payload]`, trusted constructors, `closure?`/`continuation?` type tests, `payload` and `owner` accessors.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:33:- **Cell, stream and cursor refs stay sealed plain data.** They name table entries, are already unforgeable, and must remain usable as dict keys. I withdraw my mob round-1 remark that cell refs should become host types.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:41:| Construction (`ast_walker.cljc:149`, `:545`; `semantic.cljc:306`, `:357`; `stack.cljc:548`, `:702`; `register.cljc:561`, `:713`; the four `lower-closure`s) | Wrap the existing map: `(value/closure owner payload)`. |\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:42:| Application (`ast_walker.cljc:266`, `:634`, `:677`; `semantic.cljc:231`; `stack.cljc:574`; `register.cljc:585`) | `(value/closure? f)` then read fields from `(value/payload f)`. A type test replaces a keyword compare. |\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:45:| Encoder (`engine.cljc:656-690`) | Type tests come before the `map?` arm. A plain map with `:type :closure` is now a literal and is wrapped as one. |\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:46:| Completion (`completion.cljc:89-110`) | `typed?` on closures and continuations becomes type tests over the payload. |\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:48:| Printing (`repl.cljc:317-331`) | Render from the payload, never the owner tag. |\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:50:**Two-mode trace (closes gemini concern 1).** Kernel-shape interpretation applies only when the tracer arrives from a kernel root, an engine table, or a host-typed payload. Once it crosses into a value position (an environment value, an evaluated operand, a register, a cell's content), every map is walked whole as data. A guest map shaped like a walker frame is then never pruned.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:52:**Gemini concern 2** stays a rule, not code: primitives return realized persistent data, never lazy sequences or host closures over guest values.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:57:- Equality and hash need per-host protocol blocks: `Object`/`IHashEq` on CLJ, `IEquiv`/`IHash` on CLJS and CLJD. Order the reader conditional with `:cljd` first.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:69:| `:foreign-value` | A host-typed value whose owner is not this task. Carries `:kind :closure` or `:continuation`. |\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:73:One engine function classifies the operator; the four kernels call it. After D7 a malformed in-machine continuation cannot exist, so there is no structural validation to write, which is what I argued in the mob.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:75:## Q4. Task ownership\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:77:- **Why it is needed even with unforgeable types:** a genuine `Closure` from task A can reach task B raw through an in-memory stream (`handle-put` appends the value as given, `engine.cljc:435-449`). Its program counters, segment ids and cell refs are A's coordinates.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:78:- **Check:** owner compared at application and invocation, `identical?` first, `=` as fallback. One field read on the hot path.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:79:- **Tasks without a secret** get owner `nil` and accept only owner-`nil` values.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:80:- **Lift is the only crossing.** The encoder refuses a foreign-owned value as `:yin.k/non-portable`; `lower-closure` mints with the receiver's owner. Install children have their own secrets, hence their own tags.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:82:- **In-task streams:** a closure passed through a stream inside one task still works; the reclamation pin logic must trace through wrappers.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:86:**Structural, as today.** `=` on two host-typed values compares kind, owner and payload.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:91:- Hash must agree with equality so closures can sit in sets and as map keys.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:97:1. **Forged closure or continuation map carrying the key** (mob finding). Closed by D7.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:99:3. **Wire marker naming another module's store** (by reading, not executed). `lower-closure` copies `:yin.k/store-of` from the marker (`semantic.cljc:1017`, `:1034`; `ast_walker.cljc:1191-1199`; `register.cljc:852-853`). A module's slice could name a store the receiver already holds, and \"first link wins\" keeps the live victim store. **Fix:** at lower, a closure may name store `m` only if its origin image belongs to manifest `m`. This is a linker check.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:101:**Structural follow-up:** on the named kernels the store context lives in the lexical environment under a keyword. Moving it onto the closure and return frame, as the positional kernels already do (`engine.cljc:130-137`), removes entrance 2 by construction. I recommend the parameter refusal now and the move later.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:105:**Slice A (D6 + D7 core, one slice across four VMs):** `yin.vm.value`; construction, recognition and unpacking in each kernel; encoder, completion, trace, printing; owner tag; the four refusal reasons; non-symbol parameter refusal.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:113:- Rows 3 and 4 answer nil; `assoc` on a closure does not yield an applicable value.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:115:- A closure put on an in-memory stream by one task and applied by another refuses with `:foreign-value`; the same closure through lift and lower works.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:116:- `=` on two closures from one lambda with equal environments is true; closure versus look-alike map is false; hash agrees.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:121:**Spike prelude: one required change.** `py/numeric?` classifies by elimination and tests `(nil? (get x :type))` (`prelude.cljc:74-87`). After D7 a closure answers nil there and would be classified as a number. Add two pure predicates to the `data` module, `number?` and `callable?`, and rewrite `py/numeric?` on `data/number?`. `py/cell?` (`prelude.cljc:66`) keeps working because cell refs stay maps. The lowering does not change.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:125:1. **Equality:** structural (recommended) or identity.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:127:3. **Named-kernel store context:** parameter refusal now and structural move later (recommended), or the move inside slice A.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:134:| high | `src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:266`, `engine.cljc:60-62` | Rows 1 and 2: forged closure and continuation maps are applied. | Slice A. |\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:135:| high | `src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:149`, `:545` | Rows 3 and 4: `get` reads `:env` out of any closure or continuation, exposing captured cell refs. | Host types without `ILookup`. |\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:137:| medium, by reading | `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:1017-1034`, `register.cljc:852-853` | `lower-closure` trusts the marker's store name. | Slice B origin check. |\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790849347441-architect-d7-host-typed-closures-continuations.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:138:| medium | `src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:74-87` | `py/numeric?` depends on closures answering `:type`. | `data/number?`. |\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:14:            [clojure.string :as str]\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:16:            [dao.stream :as stream]\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:17:            [dao.stream.ringbuffer :as ring]\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:42:            [dao.stream.observer :as observer]))\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:52:   shell is the only appender and its one step owner serializes evaluation, so\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:87:   value the REPL prints whole, so a result past a thousand rows is a\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:97:   hold a long string literal.  The byte bound keeps every answer on the\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:133:  (str \"the evaluation called dao.space.query/q more than \"\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:138:(def vm-constructors\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:165:   its first instruction.  A closure a previous input stored (a `def`)\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:170:   and keeps `:hash` the canonical H of the whole `:segment`\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:178:           :pc (stack/absolute-pc attached [(dcode/image-hash image) 0])\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:190:   the first does, and `:hash` is the canonical R of that concatenation.\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:195:        pc (register/absolu