rc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/summary.md:147:- ✨ Distributed AST transformation\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/summary.md:164:| Distribution | 🔄 Foundation laid |\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/summary.md:177:This implementation demonstrates:\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/summary.md:180:- How persistent data structures enable elegant state management\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:3:This document incrementally describes the Universal AST (Abstract Syntax Tree)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:5:structure that represents all executable code as immutable data.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:64:**Purpose:** Represents a constant value (number, string, boolean, nil, etc.)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:170:- In the Universal AST, literals from different source languages all compile to this same structure:\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:182:global store, primitive operations, or the module registry.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:225:4. **Module registry** — only reached for a namespaced name (e.g. `stream/put!`);\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:226:   resolved by `yin.vm.module/resolve-module` against the registry value the\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:234:  [env store primitives registry name]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:243:                              registry\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:244:                              (symbol (str (namespace name) \".\" (clojure.core/name name)))))]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:246:          (fail (str \"Unable to resolve symbol: \" name \" in this context\")\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:259:- 🔍 **Lookup-based** - Requires searching an environment, store, primitive table, or module registry\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:262:- 🧩 **Module-backed** - A namespaced name that misses env/store/primitives resolves through a composition-supplied registry value, not a global\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:355:- **Equal by structure.** `=` compares kind, owner and payload, and `hash` agrees, so two runs of one program give equal VM states and a closure can be a set member. A closure never equals its payload map.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:391:- `:tail?` - (Optional) read by the lowering profile that flattens this tree into an instruction vector (`yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` §5.2.1); the walker's own `:application` arm does not branch on it — a CESK machine's continuation stack already reuses the frame for a tail call.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:574:**Purpose:** To generate a unique identifier (symbol or string).\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:579: :prefix <string>}  ; Optional: prefix for the generated ID\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:582:**Evaluation:** Increments an internal counter and returns a unique string starting with the prefix (default `\"id\"`).\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:682:- **Roots:** the kernel's registers (`IModuleKernel/gc-roots`), the store and every module store, `:parked`, `:wait-set`, `:ready-queue`, the value being boxed, and every pinned cell. Code, images, the registry, primitives and `:resources` are not roots.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:684:- **Pinning:** a cell ref inside a value appended by `:stream/put`, or inside FFI request arguments, is pinned for the task's life, because the VM cannot see inside a medium.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:705:**Evaluation:** Returns a continuation value carrying the current continuation and lexical environment: a `yin.vm.values/Continuation` host type, minted only by a kernel and owned by the capturing task, over the `:reified-continuation` payload map. Like a closure (Part 3) it is unforgeable, opaque to guest `get`, equal by structure, and refused with `:foreign-value` in another task. A continuation never crosses a lift (`:non-canonicalizable`). A `:parked-continuation` stays plain data: resume goes by id through the engine's `:parked` table.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:792:- 🚦 **Concurrency** - Foundation for the Yin VM's cooperative multitasking and stream-based IO.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:799:Stream operations model all IO as data over `dao.stream`. Every stream op\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:802:outcome is ready — there is no ambient waiter registration, and no operation\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:804:`docs/design/dao.stream.md`).\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:808:**Type:** `:stream/make`\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:814:{:type :stream/make\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:815: :buffer <long>}  ; Optional: capacity of the stream buffer\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:820::stream/make (let [capacity (or (:buffer node) vm/default-stream-capacity)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:821:                   effect (module/make-effect :stream/make {:capacity capacity})\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:829:**Type:** `:stream/put`\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:831:**Purpose:** To send a value into a stream.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:835:{:type :stream/put\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:836: :target <ast-node> ; The stream to write to\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:848::stream/put (cesk-return state (:target node) env\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:849:                         {:frame node, :next k, :env env, :type :eval-stream-put-target}\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:853::eval-stream-put-target\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:855:      stream-ref (:value state)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:858:              (assoc k :type :eval-stream-put-val :stream-ref stream-ref)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:859:              stream-ref))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:862::eval-stream-put-val\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:864:      stream-ref (:stream-ref k)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:865:      effect (module/make-effect :stream/put {:stream stream-ref, :val val})\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:869:         :park-entry-fns {:stream/put (fn [_s _e r]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:871:                                         :stream-id (:stream-id r), :datom val})}})]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:879:**Type:** `:stream/cursor`\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:881:**Purpose:** To mint a reading position (opaque cursor) on a stream.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:885:{:type :stream/cursor\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:886: :source <ast-node> ; The stream to read from\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:892::stream/cursor (cesk-return state (:source node) env\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:893:                            {:frame node, :next k, :env env, :type :eval-stream-cursor-source}\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:896::eval-stream-cursor-source\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:897:(let [stream-ref (:value state)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:898:      effect (module/make-effect :stream/cursor {:stream stream-ref})\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:906:**Type:** `:stream/next`\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:908:**Purpose:** To read the next value at a stream cursor.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:912:{:type :stream/next\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:918:is available on the stream at that cursor, then returns that value. The\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:924::stream/next (cesk-return state (:source node) env\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:925:                          {:frame node, :next k, :env env, :type :eval-stream-next-cursor}\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:928::eval-stream-next-cursor\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:930:      effect (module/make-effect :stream/next {:cursor cursor-ref})\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:934:         :park-entry-fns {:stream/next (fn [_s _e r]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:936:                                          :cursor-ref (:cursor-ref r), :stream-id (:stream-id r)})}})]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:944:**Type:** `:stream/close`\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:950:{:type :stream/close\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:951: :source <ast-node> ; The stream to close\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:956:> The walker's `case` has no `:stream/close` arm — evaluating this node\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:959:> `:eval-stream-cursor-source` above, raising `(module/make-effect :stream/close\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:960:> {:stream ref})` through `engine/handle-effect`), and its own\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:968:- 🧱 **Parking, not blocking** - `:stream/put` and `:stream/next` park the calling continuation in the VM's wait set; no host thread blocks and nothing registers a waiter with the transport.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:969:- 🔗 **Decoupling** - Producers and consumers communicate through streams, not direct calls.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:970:- ⚠️ **`:stream/close` is speculative** - documented in the vocabulary, absent from the evaluator, until U1 of the tuples plan lands.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:977:> evaluator, and never will be.** `yin.vm.ast-walker`'s own docstring\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:986:Macro expansion is a compile-time (or load-time) stream transformation, completely external to the evaluator, that consumes unexpanded AST and produces the canonical tuples the walker receives. `docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md` specifies the expander itself, and `src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc` contains the implementation. No macro node or macro-expand tag ever reaches the tuple grammar.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:988:When it is built, it will run upstream of the walker, on a syntax medium the\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:994:- 🏗️ **Structural Transformation** - Macros manipulate code structure, not just values, before any evaluator sees the result.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:1000:## Part 10: Special Application (dao.stream.apply)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:1002:The `:dao.stream.apply/call` node represents a cross-process FFI call: it\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:1003:sends a request over an outbound stream to a host-side bridge and parks the\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:1009:**Type:** `:dao.stream.apply/call`\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:1011:**Purpose:** To perform an asynchronous, stream-based function call across\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:1016:{:type :dao.stream.apply/call\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:1025:2. **Park and Request** - `park-and-call` parks the continuation (`engine/park-continuation`) *before* touching the transport — deliberately, so an error raised later cannot strand a continuation with a consumed id — then encodes and appends a request naming `:op` and the evaluated arguments to the bridge's inbound (`call-in`) stream.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:1027:4. **Await Response** - The continuation stays parked until a correlated reply lands on the bridge's outbound (`call-out`) stream (`:dao.stream.apply/eval-call`), at which point `ffi/call-result` extracts the return value and the continuation resumes with it.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:1031::dao.stream.apply/call\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:1038:                 :next k, :env env, :type :dao.stream.apply/eval-operand}\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:1044:        response-cont {:type :dao.stream.apply/eval-call, :next k, :env env}\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:1049:    (case (:dao.stream/outcome result)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:1050:      :dao.stream/ok\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:1055:      :dao.stream/full\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:1058:                  {:k {:type :dao.stream.apply/request-sent, :parked-id parked-id,\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:1060:                   :env env, :reason :put, :stream-id vm/call-in-stream-key, :datom request})\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:1063:                      {:op op, :outcome (:dao.stream/outcome result)})))))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:1069:- 📡 **Message-Based, Retry-Safe** - Operates by sending and receiving discrete requests/responses over streams; a `full` append is retried by the wait set, never silently dropped.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:1070:- 🧱 **Parking, not blocking** - The calling continuation parks like any other stream operation; nothing invokes a callback from inside the transport, and no host thread blocks.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:1086:  instruction vector (Part 4); the walker's own evaluation does not branch on it.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/ast.md:1103:The simplicity of literals demonstrates the power of the Universal AST - even the most basic values are represented uniformly as data.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/assignments.md:24:print(x)  # 6\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/assignments.md:34:    (print x)))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/assignments.md:48:               :operator {:type :variable :name 'print}\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/assignments.md:362:Use **different strategies for different contexts**:\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:14:| `stream/next` (parking) | `ast_walker.cljc:143-164` | Parks the continuation in the wait-set when a stream is empty. |\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:15:| `stream/put` (parking) | `ast_walker.cljc:104-126` | Parks the continuation in the wait-set when a stream is at capacity. |\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:16:| Cooperative scheduler | `ast_walker.cljc:469-612` | `check-wait-set` promotes entries to `run-queue` when stream conditions clear. `resume-from-run-queue` restores them as the active computation. |\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:23:Streams are the natural yield mechanism. `put` on a full stream parks. `next` on an empty stream parks. The scheduler resumes parked computations when the stream condition clears. Two computations sharing streams form a coroutine pair.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:28:stream S (capacity 1)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:40:Two streams, each capacity 1:\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:43:stream A->B (capacity 1)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:44:stream B->A (capacity 1)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:54:Each coroutine yields by doing `put` then `next`. The stream capacity enforces turn-taking.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:56:**Bootstrap:** The first coroutine must seed a value before blocking. If A does `next` on an empty stream as its first action, it parks, and nobody is running to start B. Solution: A puts first, then reads. Or: drive the bootstrap from the host via separate `load-program`/`run` cycles (see model 2).\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:59:- No special primitives beyond streams\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:66:`:vm/park` suspends the current computation and returns the parked continuation descriptor as the VM's final value. The host (or a higher-level scheduler) inspects this value, extracts the park-id, and constructs a `:vm/resume` AST node for the next cycle. VM state carries across `load-program`/`run` cycles.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:91:- No bootstrap problem (host drives each cycle)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:95:**Trade-off:** `:vm/resume` currently takes static `:parked-id` and `:val` fields baked into the AST node. The host constructs these after seeing the park result, so this works. For in-VM resume with runtime-computed park-ids, `:vm/resume` would need to evaluate its arguments as sub-expressions (same pattern as `:stream/put` evaluating `:target` and `:val`). This is a behavioral change to an existing node type, not a new node type.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:114:The continuation is a first-class value. It can be stored, passed through streams, or sent to another node (via datom migration). The combination of capture + store + park + resume gives the same power as Scheme's `call/cc`.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:141:| Bootstrap | Seed initial value | Host drives cycles | Host or self-bootstrapping |\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:143:| Topology | Fixed at stream creation | Host-determined | Dynamic |\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:148:All three models connect to the pi-calculus. Streams are channels. Putting a stream-ref through a stream is channel mobility (name passing). Coroutines communicating via streams are pi-calculus processes communicating via channels.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:150:The stream-based model maps directly: each coroutine is a process, each stream is a channel, `put`/`next` are send/receive, capacity is synchronization.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:152:Park/resume adds explicit process suspension, analogous to the pi-calculus restriction operator (creating a private channel/continuation).\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/co-routines.md:158:All three models work today with the AST Walker VM. The other VMs (Stack, Register, Semantic) need the primitives added first (see `docs/continuation-stream-feature-parity-across-vms.md`).\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/formal_tests_summary.md:107:- ✅ Literals (numbers, strings)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/formal_tests_summary.md:131:5. **Documentation** - Inline comments explain AST structures\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/formal_tests_summary.md:149:### Test Infrastructure\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/formal_tests_summary.md:158:2. **Organized structure** - Proper `test/` directory layout\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/bytecode-vm-optimization.md:11:The bytecode VMs (RegisterVM, StackVM) use persistent immutable data structures throughout execution. Every instruction:\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/bytecode-vm-optimization.md:13:2. Allocates a new state map via `assoc`: new persistent map nodes per instruction\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/bytecode-vm-optimization.md:14:3. Updates registers/stack via `assoc`/`conj`: new persistent vector nodes per instruction\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/bytecode-vm-optimization.md:16:This negates the linear-access advantage bytecode VMs have over the AST walker. Both execution models produce the same allocation-dominated profile. The pointer chasing moved from AST node traversal into persistent data structure internals.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/bytecode-vm-optimization.md:76:### Instruction Implementations\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/bytecode-vm-optimization.md:78:Simple ops (`:literal`, `:load-var`, `:move`, `:branch`, `:jump`): `arr-get` for reads, `arr-set!` for register writes, `vswap! vpc` for pc advance, `recur` to continue loop. Zero allocation per instruction.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/bytecode-vm-optimization.md:110:Called only at boundaries: effects, blocking, halting, parking, reification. Not called per instruction.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/bytecode-vm-optimization.md:163:- **`env`**: closures capture env, structural sharing is essential\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/bytecode-vm-optimization.md:175:Add `reg-vm-run-active-continuation` to `register.cljc`. Start with common opcodes (`:literal`, `:load-var`, `:move`, `:branch`, `:jump`, `:return`), then add `:lambda`, `:call`, `:tailcall`, then stream/continuation ops. Wire `reg-vm-eval` to use it.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/bytecode-vm-optimization.md:192:- But instructions between calls are allocation-free\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/bytecode-vm-optimization.md:429:     time in the allocation-free instruction loop.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/bytecode-vm-optimization.md:439:   - The scheduler layer pays a high per-instruction tax for `atom` updates and\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/bytecode-vm-optimization.md:441:     instruction set.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/bytecode-vm-optimization.md:448:     destructure this wrapper before accessing the native list.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/bytecode-vm-optimization.md:514:### Instruction Throughput\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/bytecode-vm-optimization.md:516:The tail-countdown benchmark executes ~8 bytecode instructions per loop iteration\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/bytecode-vm-optimization.md:519:- ~400,000 bytecode instructions in 113ms (CLJD)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/bytecode-vm-optimization.md:520:- ~3.5 million instructions/sec\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/bytecode-vm-optimization.md:524:At 60fps the frame budget is 16.6ms. Estimated instruction capacity per frame:\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/bytecode-vm-optimization.md:534:  instructions): easily within a single frame on either path.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/bytecode-vm-optimization.md:536:  2,000-10,000 instructions): fine if not every frame.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/bytecode-vm-optimization.md:565:This is the same mechanism used for effects and stream operations, applied to\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/bytecode-vm-optimization.md:566:frame-budget management. No additional infrastructure is needed.\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/documentation_index.md:33:- ✅ **Part 1: Literals** - Simple values (numbers, strings, booleans, collections)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/docs/documentation_index.md:128:  - Te